186045417] [17592186065317 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2f58c76e-8d14-575a-b5de-62cc2003eb38" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065317 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065318 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065320 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065318 #edb/ref 1061 907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065318 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a12978db-32b6-52fc-a426-abfda5e434ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065318 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065319 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065318 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065319 #edb/ref 1061 910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065319 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9c671f5f-a9e8-5694-8457-ab3f9df8b239" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065319 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064380 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065320 #edb/ref 1061 913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065320 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "318a88e0-e4a9-5b31-a8c3-9cb7712ff53b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065320 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064381 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065384 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we're told that the radius of a circle is **growing at a rate of $2\\,\\textrm{cm}$ every second**, as shown below.\n\n![](images/27/27d0543834333202411a9700401b7e33329a255c7103c9f2ebe394d8c1cf694b.png)\n\nIf the radius of the circle is growing, then its area is also increasing. How do we calculate the **rate at which the area is increasing**?\n\nThe rate at which the area is changing can be expressed using a derivative:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}\n$$\n\nHere, $A$ is the area of the circle, measured in $\\textrm{cm}^2,$ and $t$ is the time in seconds. \n\nWe know that the area is related to the radius $r$ as\n\n$$\nA = \\pi r^2.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}r}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\pi r\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the radius of a circle is growing at a rate of $2\\,\\textrm{cm}$ every second, we have $\\dfrac{\\textrm dr}{\\textrm dt} = 2 \\, \\textrm{cm}.$ Substituting this information, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\cdot 2\\pi r=4\\pi r\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd, that's it, we're done! \n\nIf we want to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$ at a particular moment, we substitute the value of $r$ at that moment into the above. So, for example, if we want to compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t}$ at the moment when $r=6\\,\\textrm{cm},$ we plug this into the above and get\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d A}{\\textrm d t} = 4\\pi \\cdot 6 = 24\\pi\\,\\textrm{cm}^2/\\textrm{s}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065384 #edb/ref 1065 488 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065384 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6925e269-127a-5cf1-8116-aa809d1cf42f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065384 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065396 #edb/ref 1064 "When a function $y=y(x)$ contains complex products, quotients, or radicals, we can use logarithmic differentiation to find the derivative $y'(x)$.\n\nFor example, consider the following function:\n\n$$\ny = x^2 \\cdot 2^x\n$$\n\nOur goal is to find $y'(x)$. To do this, we will use the product rule for logarithms, namely\n\n$$\n\\ln(a b)= \\ln a + \\ln b\n$$\n\nWe differentiate our function by following four steps.\n\n**Step 1.** We take the natural logarithm of both sides and apply the product and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln y &= \\ln (x^{2} \\cdot 2^{x}) \\\\\n\\ln y &= \\ln (x^{2}) + \\ln (2^{x}) \\\\\n\\ln y &= 2\\ln x + x\\ln 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2.** Next, we differentiate both sides of our equation using implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\ln y) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(2\\ln x + x\\ln 2) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot y^{′} &= 2 \\cdot (\\ln x)^{′} + (x)^{′} \\cdot \\ln (2) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot y^{′} &= 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{x} + 1 \\cdot \\ln (2) \\\\\n\\frac{y^{′}}{y} &= \\frac{2}{x} + \\ln 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Then, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= y(\\frac{2}{x} + \\ln 2)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 4.** Finally, we substitute back our original expression for\n\n$$\ny =x^2\\cdot 2^x{:}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= x^{2} \\cdot 2^{x}(\\frac{2}{x} + \\ln 2) \\\\\n&= x \\cdot 2^{x + 1} + x^{2} \\cdot 2^{x}\\ln 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065396 #edb/ref 1065 6088 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065396 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1895a7f3-5ba4-5a31-85b3-6a7b050b307c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065396 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065397 #edb/ref 1064 "In cases where we wish to differentiate a quotient of functions\n\n$$\ny = f(x) = \\dfrac{g(x)}{h(x)}\n$$\n\nwe can apply the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\ln \\left(\\dfrac{a}{b}\\right) = \\ln a - \\ln b\n$$\n\nFor example, let's compute $y'$, where the function $y$ is given by\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac {e^{x^2}}{(1-2x)^2(x-1)^3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\ln y &= \\ln ((e^{x^{2}})/((1 - 2x)^{2}(x - 1)^{3})) \\\\\n\\ln y &= \\ln (e^{x^{2}}) - \\ln ((1 - 2x)^{2}) - \\ln ((x - 1)^{3}) \\\\\n\\ln y &= x^{2} - 2\\ln (1 - 2x) - 3\\ln (x - 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{d}{dx}(\\ln y) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(x^{2} - 2\\ln (1 - 2x) - 3\\ln (x - 1)) \\\\\n\\frac{1}{y} \\cdot y^{′} &= 2x - 2 \\cdot \\frac{1}{1 - 2x} \\cdot (-2) - 3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{x - 1} \\\\\n\\frac{y^{′}}{y} &= 2x + \\frac{4}{1 - 2x} - \\frac{3}{x - 1}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve for $y'{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= y(2x + \\frac{4}{1 - 2x} - \\frac{3}{x - 1})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute our expression for $y$, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny^{′} &= (e^{x^{2}})/((1 - 2x)^{2}(x - 1)^{3})(2x + \\frac{4}{1 - 2x} - \\frac{3}{x - 1}) \\\\\n&= (e^{x^{2}})/((1 - 2x)^{2}(x - 1)^{3})((2x(1 - 2x)(x - 1) + 4(x - 1) - 3(1 - 2x))/((1 - 2x)(x - 1))) \\\\\n&= (e^{x^{2}})/((1 - 2x)^{3}(x - 1)^{4})(2x(1 - 2x)(x - 1) + 4(x - 1) - 3(1 - 2x)) \\\\\n&= (e^{x^{2}})/((1 - 2x)^{3}(x - 1)^{4})(-4x^{3} + 6x^{2} + 8x - 7) \\\\\n&= - (e^{x^{2}})/((1 - 2x)^{3}(x - 1)^{4})(4x^{3} - 6x^{2} - 8x + 7) \\\\\n&= - (e^{x^{2}}(4x^{3} - 6x^{2} - 8x + 7))/((1 - 2x)^{3}(x - 1)^{4})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065397 #edb/ref 1065 6090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065397 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "8cf6e625-669a-5fda-b756-e1ce091eaa93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065397 #edb/ref 1067 "Using Logarithms to Differentiate a Quotient" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078407 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078407 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049362 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078407 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078407 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078407 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186078958 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078407 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186079002 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078407 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234076 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078407 #edb/ref 1039 "Recognizing Derivatives in Limits Similar to the Instantaneous Rate of Change Formula" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078407 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186078279 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078407 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3f5c089a-9506-5a0e-83ae-48efa8fd314c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078408 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186078408 #edb/ref 103